    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "it just feels so sneaky lol. like i'm paying this much money but the debt barely moves. is there any way to get ahead of it or is the system just designed to make sure the interest stays high as long as possible?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Fastest way to get ahead of it: **pay more than the minimum** — every extra dollar goes straight to principal (make sure your servicer applies it that way, more on that in a sec). Two other effective moves:\n\n- **Pay biweekly instead of monthly** — you sneak in 13 payments a year instead of 12, or\n- **Refinance** to a lower rate if your credit is good, though you lose federal protections\n\n**The critical part:** watch for \"payment advance\" — some servicers apply extra payments as an *early payment on next month's bill* instead of toward principal, which keeps you stuck in the same amortization. Look for the option to \"pay as direct\" or specify \"apply to principal.\"\n\nSo is it designed to favor the lender? The math is just how amortization works — but yes, the default setup benefits them, not you. The good news is extra principal payments compound in your favor the same way interest was working against you.",
